# Package

This page is how you ship a working app. Field tables stay in
[Configuration](/docs/reference/configuration/). Rocci is **development**
`main`. Production signing, notarization, and OS installers are not shipped.

**Verify:** `rocci validate` succeeds; `build --release` writes a server tree.

## Configure and secure

Unknown `rocci.toml` fields are errors. Validate before you change anything:

```sh
cargo run -q -p rocci-cli -- validate
```

`[http].host` is loopback only (`127.0.0.1` / `localhost`). Origins and CSP
belong in the configuration reference. Do not treat the complete-shape example
as canonical — the field tables are.

## Release server

```sh
cargo run -q -p rocci-cli -- build --release examples/rocci/standalone/counter
cargo run -q -p rocci-cli -- build --release examples/rocci/standalone/counter --target x64musl
```

This packages a Linux **server** process plus `assets/`, not a macOS `.app`.
`--target` requires `--release`. Run the artifact behind your own reverse
proxy; do not expose the preview window as production.

Container sketches live under `docker/` (`rocci-ops serve app`). They consume
the release tree; they do not invent a new install channel.

## Ad-hoc macOS `.app`

```sh
cargo run -q -p rocci-cli -- bundle --config examples/rocci/standalone/counter/rocci.toml
```

Host-native only. `--target` is rejected for `.app`. The bundle is ad-hoc
signed. Standalone `.rocci` versus a directory with `main.roc` is the same
packaging choice as [custom](/docs/applications/custom/): generated dispatch
versus owned routing.

**Related:** [Compatibility](/docs/reference/compatibility/),
[CLI](/docs/reference/cli/).
